#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162955} :activity/id #uuid "47f06722-f795-54cf-9c4e-60c1b8ef204c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050755 :topic/id #uuid "63e066cd-f668-558d-b525-f3e275539d65" :topic/math-academy-id 3077} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162955 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163265 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, X_3$ drawn from a population where $X_i\\sim B(5,\\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown probability of success. Let's assume we conducted our sample and got the following data:\n\n$$\nx_1 = 4, \\qquad x_2 = 1, \\qquad x_3 = 1\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the log-likelihood function for this sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = \\ln 125 + 6 \\ln \\theta +9\\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nOur goal is to form an estimate for the unknown parameter $\\theta$. To do this, we find the value of $\\theta$ that maximizes the probability of getting the observed sample data. In other words, we need to maximize the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$. And, since the natural logarithm is an increasing function, a value of $\\theta$ that maximizes the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$ will also maximize the log-likelihood function $l(\\theta)$.\n\nEstimates of an unknown parameter $\\theta$ obtained by maximizing the likelihood function (or, equivalently, the log-likelihood function) are called **maximum likelihood estimates**. We'll use the usual $\\hat{\\theta}$ notation to denote a maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$.\n\n![](images/00/005367bc78937c5bd5b684cb260aff951de2e85d7c2907bb6e11c312ad4f24bd.png)\n\nTo find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$, we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dl}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\ln 125 + 6\\ln θ + 9\\ln (1 - θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{θ} + \\frac{9}{1 - θ} \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{θ} + \\frac{9}{θ - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6}{θ} + \\frac{9}{θ - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(6(θ - 1) + 9θ)/(θ(θ - 1)) &= 0 \\\\\n6(θ - 1) + 9θ &= 0 \\\\\n15θ - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n15θ &= 6 \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{6}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} = \\dfrac{6}{15}\n$$\n\n**Note**: It's easy to check that this is a maximum of $l(\\theta)$ by calculating the second derivative. We'll omit this step." :tutorial/id #uuid "e0b84f42-4629-5b2d-8d13-fa63702d90c1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5363 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b7f787d2-b4e8-502a-8321-04c9a97d4ddb" :step/math-academy-id 33493 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162115 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161681 :question/id #uuid "ce0d131b-ec10-5895-942f-2bece346c152" :question/math-academy-id "e-10107"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62aa30b-6bf5-5c06-bb1f-8269a6806368" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162454}] :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Discrete Probability Distributions"} :step/id #uuid "437bea9e-1d36-5907-8e57-9f034069ce0e" :step/math-academy-id 38124 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163085}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162116 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161682 :question/id #uuid "dd2ad84f-1fa2-5f7c-9af3-db9dce4b5133" :question/math-academy-id "e-10108"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78241be4-1db7-554c-a7ce-7532345f3f5a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049417}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162456}] :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Continuous Probability Distributions"} :step/id #uuid "91727a9a-1b8e-51a5-b665-f051db61e0dd" :step/math-academy-id 38125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163361 :tutorial/content "Let's derive a general expression for the maximum likelihood estimator for the unknown parameter $\\theta$ for a set of $n$ independent and identically distributed Bernoulli random variables.\n\nSuppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{n}$ is an I.I.D random sample, where $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{n}$, the log-likelihood function is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = S\\ln\\theta + (n-S)\\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nis the number of successes in the sample.\n\nTo compute a general expression for the maximum likelihood estimate, we differentiate $l(\\theta)$, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dl}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(S\\ln θ + (n - S)\\ln (1 - θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{S}{θ} + \\frac{n - S}{1 - θ} \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{S}{θ} + \\frac{n - S}{θ - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{S}{θ} + \\frac{n - S}{θ - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(S(θ - 1) + (n - S)θ)/(θ(θ - 1)) &= 0 \\\\\nS(θ - 1) + (n - S)θ &= 0 \\\\\nSθ - S + nθ - Sθ &= 0 \\\\\nnθ - S &= 0 \\\\\nnθ &= S \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{S}{n} \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nwhich is simply the *mean of the sample*.\n\nThe maximum likelihood estimate for other common distributions can be derived similarly. Let's summarize the most important cases." :tutorial/id #uuid "02b1561b-e05d-5bc8-b9f8-84197c27336e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7009 :tutorial/title "Deriving a Maximum Likelihood Estimator for Bernoulli Distributions"} :step/id #uuid "ce955c2b-ae6e-5a1f-a69e-b47345d71769" :step/math-academy-id 42311 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163362 :tutorial/content "The table below summarizes the maximum likelihood estimates for several common probability distributions. These results can be derived by maximizing the corresponding log-likelihood function.\n\n| **Distribution** | **Probability Mass Function, $f(x; \\theta)$** | **Parameter, $\\theta$** | **Maximum Likelihood Estimate, $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Bernoulli | $\\displaystyle \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$<br>The mean of the sample |\n| Binomial | $\\displaystyle {N \\choose x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{N-x}$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$<br>The mean of the sample |\n| Poisson | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\theta^{x} e^{-\\theta}}{x!}$ | The rate parameter | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$<br>The mean of the sample |\n| Geometric | $\\displaystyle (1-\\theta)^{x-1} \\theta$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\bigg)^{-1}$<br>The reciprocal of the sample mean |\n| Exponential | $\\displaystyle \\theta e^{-\\theta x}$ | The rate parameter | $\\displaystyle \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\bigg)^{-1}$<br>The reciprocal of the sample mean |\n| Normal | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{\\theta_2 \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{x-\\theta_1}{\\theta_2} \\right)^2}$ | The mean $(\\theta_1)$<br><br>The standard deviation $(\\theta_2)$ | $\\displaystyle \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}_1 = \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$<br>The mean of the sample<br><br>$\\displaystyle \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}_2 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2}$<br>The standard deviation of the sample |" :tutorial/id #uuid "f53294df-24ec-5dd3-a997-b4ac32b6aa6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7020 :tutorial/title "A List of Important Maximum Likelihood Estimators"} :step/id #uuid "06c8ad6b-ddb9-55a3-b7c6-7bc3252ba7dc" :step/math-academy-id 42322}] :activity/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162205}]}
